{"agent":"main","body":{"claimed_done":true,"claimed_done_reason":"structural","final_message_bytes":1233,"final_message_hash":"sha256:09f9289416b9a2cfae71ed9ba4717954c413715966826ddafe1970926026ae6b","final_message_inline":"The bug was in the code, not the test.\n\n**Diagnosis:** `shardFor` used `hashKey(key) % shards`. JavaScript's `%` keeps the sign of the dividend, so any key whose folded 32-bit FNV-1a hash is negative (about half of all keys) returned a negative shard index. The \"live assignments do not move\" test never caught it because all 20 fixture keys happen to hash non-negative; the range test generates random keys, so it failed whenever it drew a negative-hashing key — roughly one run in two. The test was asserting exactly the documented contract (README: \"the non-negative remainder … the same value Python's `%` gives\"), so it stayed untouched.\n\n**Fix** in `src/shard.ts:17`:\n\n```ts\n// JS `%` keeps the sign of the dividend, so fold it back into [0, shards).\nreturn ((hashKey(key) % shards) + shards) % shards;\n```\n\n**Verification:**\n- Suite run three times in a row: `# fail 0` each time.\n- No key moves: all 20 fixture assignments still match, and every fixture key hashes non-negative, so the change is a no-op for them — it only corrects the previously-broken negative branch.\n- `hashKey('session:1:919')` = -2110243379 → shard 13 of 16, shard 1 of 10 (the Python-`%` values).\n- `saga gate check`: G1, G2, G3 all MET.\n\nDONE","final_message_ref":null,"phase":"assistant_end"},"hash":"sha256:55f47a0d79d9c9f5fa3e1912ceb8c860a766b08273cb52b20dd9411f9248f57f","hook_ms":3,"masked_count":0,"mono_ns":45609108500,"prev":"sha256:ed13ff6bc8463e5521aa2387ba8020b8e54ebe8870af8134efee76579be75c19","schema":"saga.trace/1","seq":30,"session":"c91a8a93-3723-4350-9bb4-6a5ee54f486b","source":"hook:Stop","ts":"2026-09-13T06:33:24.240Z","turn":1,"type":"turn"}
